# Example relay inventory for scripts/roll_relays.sh (issue #399).
#
# Which relays you run is your own deployment, so this repository ships only this example. Keep
# your inventory outside the repository, one file per network, and point BINGLE_RELAYS_FILE at the
# one for the network you are rolling. The values below are placeholders.
#
# This is a data file read line by line by roll_relays.sh, not shell code: do not source it.
# Blank lines and lines starting with # are skipped, NETWORK and ACCOUNT_ID appear once, and RELAY
# repeats, one line per relay, rolled in the order listed. An unrecognised line is an error.
# Check a file with: scripts/roll_relays.sh --check-inventory <file>
#
# The file holds no secrets: each relay's passphrase is set once by the first aws/deploy_relay.sh
# and stays in its stack's Secrets Manager secret, which a roll reuses.

# testnet or mainnet; must match BINGLE_NETWORK when rolling.
NETWORK=testnet
# the AWS account the stacks are in; roll_relays.sh refuses to run signed in to any other.
ACCOUNT_ID=123456789012

# one RELAY line per relay, five fields separated by spaces:
#   <handle> <stack name> <region> <mode: ec2|express> <in-container node file>
# The stack name is normally bingle-relay-<handle> (deploy_relay.sh's default), but any existing
# stack works. The mode is the template the stack was created with: ec2 (aws/relay_stack.yaml) or
# express (aws/relay_express.yaml, Fargate). The node file is the path inside the image (/app/...)
# and must match the stack's NodeFile parameter. Each handle runs on one relay only.
RELAY=my-relay-1 bingle-relay-my-relay-1 us-east-1 ec2 /app/nodely_staging_testnet_node.json
RELAY=my-relay-2 my-relay-2 eu-west-1 express /app/nodely_staging_testnet_node.json
